{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And all the congregation of the children of Israel journeyed from the wilderness of Sin, according to their journeys, at the mouth of YHWH, and encamped in Rephidim</b> — Scripture says that they journeyed from the wilderness of Sin, where they had encamped when they set out from Elim, and they traveled through many journeys that they made at the mouth of HaShem, and after that they encamped in Rephidim. And Scripture says this in abbreviated fashion, for when they first set out from the wilderness of Sin they encamped at Dophkah, and after that at Alush, and from Alush to Rephidim. And this is the meaning of \"according to their journeys\" (לְמַסְעֵיהֶם), for there were journeys for them from the wilderness of Sin to Rephidim, and they did not arrive there in a single journey. But now Scripture comes only to explain their complaint, for at the beginning of their arrival in that wilderness they complained about bread, and now they strove about water. <b>And there was no water for the people to drink</b> — when they came to that place and found no springs of water there, they immediately made a strife with Moses. And this is the meaning of <b>and the people strove with Moses</b>, for the complaints in places where it says \"and they complained\" (וַיִּלֹּנוּ) are a murmuring — they would murmur about their situation, saying, What shall we do, what shall we eat, and what shall we drink — but \"and he strove\" (וַיָּרֶב) means they made an actual strife with him, and they came against him saying, Give us water, you and Aaron your brother, for the matter rests upon you and our blood is upon you. And Moses said to them, <b>Why do you strive with me? Why do you test YHWH?</b> — for this strife, to test YHWH, was: can you be given water? For if you fall silent before me and pray to Him, perhaps He will answer you. And so it was indeed in their hearts to test, as He said (Exodus 17:7), <b>And because they tested YHWH, saying, Is YHWH among us or not?</b>. And then their spirit weakened from upon Him, and they stood a day or two sustaining themselves with the water that was in their vessels, and after that <b>and the people thirsted there for water, and the people complained against Moses</b>, like the murmuring that they would do in every place when they sought something, saying, Why have you brought us up from Egypt? And when Moses saw that they were thirsty, he prayed to HaShem and declared before Him his trouble, concerning the strife they had made with him at first. And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra) said that there were two groups — one striving and one testing HaShem. But the correct interpretation is what I have said."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:35.282196746Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}